The '''Iskhal-Badalga South Line''' (Burawi: ''Dhaqso Iskhal-Badalga Koonfur'') is a high-speed rail line in [[Burawa]] connecting the [[South Hudur]] and [[Haro (Metropolitan Administration)|Haro]] metropolitan areas via a southern alignment. The line runs from either [[Mazar National Station]] or [[Iskhal Main Station]] to [[Badalga Central Station]], a distance of approximately 338 kilometers. The line opened in 2011 after nine years of construction, providing relief to the congested [[Iskhal-Badalga North Line]].

The Iskhal-Badalga South Line carries approximately 100 million passengers annually as of 2026, making it the second-busiest high-speed rail line in Burawa after the North Line. The line operates ten service patterns: two super express services (SX1, SX2), five express services (A, B, C, D, E), two limited services (F, G), and one all-stop local service. The line serves major business commuter pairs including Mazar-Docol, Docol-Badalga, Mazar-Khar, and Iskhal-Docol.

Despite being approximately 32 kilometers longer than the North Line, the South Line's SX2 super express service provides the fastest journey between Iskhal and Badalga. The non-stop service, operating at 350 km/h, completes the journey in approximately 50 minutes, compared to the North Line's Express A service which takes approximately 70 minutes despite the shorter distance.

== Route ==

The Iskhal-Badalga South Line runs south of the [[Buran River]], providing an alternative route to the [[Iskhal-Badalga North Line]]. Services originate from either [[Mazar National Station]] or [[Iskhal Main Station]], converging at [[Iskhal Central Station]] before continuing to [[Badalga]].

