During peak hours (6–9 AM and 4–7 PM), super express services operate every 15 minutes, express services every 10–15 minutes, and limited and local services every 20 minutes. Off-peak frequencies are reduced by approximately half.

== History ==

=== Planning and approval ===

By 2000, the [[Iskhal-Badalga North Line]] was operating significantly above capacity, with annual ridership exceeding 110 million passengers. Chronic overcrowding and delays prompted the [[BuRail]] corporation and the Burawi government to propose a parallel line to relieve congestion. The southern alignment, running south of the [[Buran River]], was selected to serve additional population centers and provide redundancy in the corridor.

=== Construction (2002–2011) ===

Construction of the Iskhal-Badalga South Line began in 2002. The project involved significant engineering challenges, including the upgrading of existing conventional railway tracks to high-speed standards and the construction of large underground sections through the Iskhal metropolitan area. The line was designed for higher speeds than the North Line, with a maximum operating speed of 350 km/h.

The line opened in 2011, nine years after construction began. The total cost was approximately 4.1 billion hanti (equivalent to approximately 8.5 billion hanti in 2026 values).

=== Induced demand ===

Following the South Line's opening, total corridor ridership continued to grow rather than split evenly between the two lines. The North Line maintained approximately 95 million annual passengers, while the South Line attracted approximately 75 million initially, growing to 100 million by 2026. This phenomenon, attributed to induced demand, reflected the improved convenience and capacity of the corridor attracting new passengers who had previously avoided rail travel due to congestion.

== Rolling stock ==

{{main|Dhaqso}}

The Iskhal-Badalga South Line is operated primarily by [[Dhaqso-V]] trainsets manufactured by the [[Burawa Train Materials Manufacturing Corporation]] (BTMMC). The Dhaqso-V is the fifth generation of the Dhaqso series, with a maximum operating speed of 350 km/h.

Standard formations consist of 12 cars with 960 seats, while extended peak-hour formations consist of 16 cars with 1,280 seats. The trainsets feature two seating classes: Commuter (2+3 dense seating) and Economy (2+2 comfortable seating).

A small number of Dhaqso-IV trainsets (320 km/h) operate on the South Line during peak hours to supplement capacity.

== Ridership ==

The Iskhal-Badalga South Line is the second-busiest railway line in Burawa. Annual ridership has grown from approximately 75 million in 2012 to over 100 million in 2026.

